  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 352 AM CDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\n     TODAY THROUGH MONDAY NIGHT...\n\nEarly this morning, water vapor satellite imagery and upper air \nanalysis continues to show a large and strong mid/upper ridge across \nthe southern/central Rockies and into the southern/central plains. \nThe bottom line: this persistent pattern continues to be very \nsupportive of hot conditions across the central plains. This \nafternoon will be much like yesterday with 500 mb heights and 850 mb \ntemperatures above the 95th percentile supporting afternoon surface \ntemperatures in the 100s across nearly the entire forecast area. \nWhile dewpoints will be lower than yesterday, they will likely \nremain in the mid 60s to low 70s. These conditions will support heat \nindices up to 110 across the forecast area, and it's possible heat \nindices could out perform what is currently forecast (much like \nyesterday). That being said, boundary layer mixing could be more \nintense than forecast for locations west of I-135 which would lead \nto dewpoints in the lower 60s. However, should this drier solution \npan out, air temperatures will likely be a tad hotter than currently \nforecast, which would still lead to heat indices approaching 110 \nacross these locations. Regardless of the finer details, it will be \nhot (yes, even for summer), and those sensitive to heat will need to \ntake the proper precautions if spending time outside this afternoon \nand early evening. Heat stress will continue even into the nighttime \nhours with lows struggling to fall below 80 degrees overnight.\n\nThe pattern is not expected to change much for Monday with the \naforementioned strong upper anti-cyclone and very warm mid-level \ntemperatures supporting hot surface air temperatures during the \nafternoon and early evening hours. Mid-range guidance continues to \nsuggest that deep mixing, mostly due to breezy south-southwest \nwinds, during the afternoon hours will likely lead to drier \nconditions on Monday; however, this will also lead to hotter \nafternoon highs. That being said, even if afternoon highs are around \n105, dewpoints in the low 60s won't drive heat indices much higher \nthan that, so a heat advisory has been maintained (no upgrade to a \nwarning) despite the forecast calling for hotter afternoon \ntemperatures than yesterday and today. \n\nThe forecast beyond Monday evening/night begins to become a little \nmore uncertain as the aforementioned upper ridge begins to break \ndown and shift westward, and a weak cold front slowly sags southward \ninto Kansas overnight Monday into Tuesday morning. A complex of \nstorms is likely to develop across the northern High Plains and \nprogress eastward into northern Kansas and southern Nebraska during \nthe overnight hours. Currently, rain chances are limited to north of \nI-70 early Tuesday morning given that very warm mid-level \ntemperatures will likely be detrimental to the maintenance of \nconvection south of I-70. With that being said, it is 2026, and the \ntrend this year has been for convection to overperform model \nguidance. Therefore, trends will need to be monitored over the next \n36-48 hours.\n\n     TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY...\n\nAssuming that convection Monday night into Tuesday morning behaves \nas currently forecast, another hot day is likely on Tuesday, \nespecially across southern and southeast Kansas. Lighter surface \nwinds out of the east/southeast Tuesday afternoon is likely to \nprevent deep mixing of the boundary layer, thus dewpoints will \nlikely a tad higher. Despite afternoon highs forecast to be slightly \n\"cooler,\" heat indices should still exceed 105 across southern and \nsoutheast Kansas Tuesday afternoon and evening. With the primary \nridge axis shifting westwards towards the Four Corners region, the \nmid-level baroclinic zone is forecast to be draped over Kansas late \nTuesday night into early Wednesday morning. Model guidance suggest \nwidely scattered showers and storms are possible along this feature \nearly Wednesday morning with some activity possibly lingering across \nfar eastern Kansas well into Wednesday afternoon. Assuming coverage \nand timing of potential convection ends up as currently forecast, \nthe area will continue to feel the influence of the ridge out west \nwith highs rising into the mid to upper 90s Wednesday afternoon. \nBeyond this point, details become a little less clear. However, in \ngeneral, mid-range model guidance suggests a pattern supportive of \nperiodic storm chances through the remainder of the week with a \nparade of shortwave troughs embedded in weak northwest flow sweeping \nover the region. Severe weather is not anticipated with this \nactivity at this time, but stay tuned for updates over the coming \ndays.",
